{# Every outside link on this page comes from `site.app` in _data/site.json, so
   the day a release is published there is one place to change.
   `site.app.download` is the Releases page, not a tag: until v0.1.0 exists
   there is no release to point at, and a tag link written early would 404.
   `site.app.signed` hides the unsigned caveat once the installers are signed. #}
<section class="wrap">
  <header class="essay-header">
    <h1>Desktop app</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The pipeline that drew every map on this site, behind a window, for
      people who do not use a terminal. It runs on macOS and Windows, and
      it is pre-alpha: expect rough edges, and expect things to change
      between releases.
    </p>
  </header>
  <p>
    Give it a city&rsquo;s published GTFS timetable and it makes the same
    things the <a href="{{ '/atlas/' | url }}">atlas</a> is made of: a
    schematic map straightened onto a 45&deg; grid, and a running animation of
    the service day across it. Then it exports them as stills, reels and GIFs
    sized for Instagram, LinkedIn, Bluesky and X. Everything happens
    on your own computer.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Add a feed</strong> from a zip on your computer, a web
      address, or the networks it already knows.</li>
    <li><strong>Inspect it</strong>: routes, agencies, the service window, and
      the network drawn where it really runs, so you can choose which mode and
      which operator to keep.</li>
    <li><strong>Lay it out</strong> with one button. The layout is computed
      once and stored, so every map and export drawn from it afterwards
      matches.</li>
    <li><strong>Style it</strong>: each line&rsquo;s colour, the order lines
      stack in, and a dark or sepia theme.</li>
    <li><strong>Export it</strong> as a still, a reel or a GIF, from presets for
      each platform.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

{# Screenshot slots: see the front matter. Nothing renders until a slot has a
   `src`, so an empty list leaves no gap and no broken image. #}
{% set shots = screenshots | selectattr("src") %}
{% for shot in shots %}
<figure class="figure" id="screenshot-{{ shot.slot }}">
  <img src="{{ ('/assets/' + shot.src) | url }}" width="{{ shot.width }}" height="{{ shot.height }}"
       loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="{{ shot.alt }}">
  <figcaption>{{ shot.caption }}</figcaption>
</figure>
{% endfor %}

<section class="wrap" id="download">
  <h2>Download</h2>
  <p>
    <strong><a href="{{ site.app.download }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Download Legible Cities from the Releases page</a></strong>,
    choosing the file for your computer from the release&rsquo;s Assets:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>a Mac with Apple silicon: the file ending in <code>-mac-arm64.dmg</code></li>
    <li>a Mac with an Intel processor: <code>-mac-x64.dmg</code></li>
    <li>a Windows PC: <code>-windows-x64-setup.exe</code></li>
  </ul>
  {% if not site.app.signed %}
  <p>
    <strong>The installers are not signed yet.</strong> The first time you open
    the app, macOS or Windows will warn you before letting it run. The
    <a href="{{ site.app.install }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">install guide</a>
    shows how to get past that warning on each system, what the app writes and
    where, and how to remove it completely.
  </p>
  {% else %}
  <p>
    The <a href="{{ site.app.install }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">install guide</a>
    walks through installing it, what the app writes and where, and how to
    remove it completely.
  </p>
  {% endif %}

  <h3>What you need</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A Mac with macOS 13 Ventura or later, Apple silicon or Intel; or a PC
      with Windows 10 or Windows 11, 64-bit.</li>
    <li>About 1&nbsp;GB of free disk space for the app, and more for the feeds
      you add and the maps and videos you make.</li>
    <li>An internet connection only to download a feed from its web address.
      Laying out a map and exporting it happen on your computer, and the app
      carries everything it runs: there is nothing else to install.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

